  "query_preview": "(Today through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nBroad and stacked high pressure will be the rule over much of the \ncentral and eastern CONUS through this weekend as an amplified H500 \nridge builds north over much of North America...before flattening \nunder zonal northern flow, to the Midwest and points south by early \nnext week.  Upper ridge axis mainly west/near IN and corresponding \nlarge surface high slowly twisting its center from Great Lakes to \nMid-Atlantic...will keep skies mainly clear, winds light while \nveering from northeast today to southerly by Sunday, humidity \nseasonably low, and temperatures slightly above normal ranging from \n40s to low 70s over most locations. Patchy pre-dawn/early morning \nfog will pose a potential impact for morning commuters.\n\nLower confidence in last third of forecast period given model \ninconsistency in handling disorganized upstream short waves across \nnorthern CONUS/southern Canada.  That said, a potent system this \nweekend spinning into Hudson Bay will likely be followed by cooler \nair mass, directly by overall zonal flow into Ontario/northern Great \nLakes. Question will be position and strength of disturbance \npossibly tracking to its south...probably across Midwest into the \nmid-week.  Higher certainty in considerable cloudiness, lower \nconfidence in any organized rain with forcing still retracted \nnorth...and perhaps a continuation of above normal afternoon highs \nnearing 70F to end the period.",
